<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shivaji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1630–<a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, Shivaji Maharaj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shivaji (1630–1680) was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> state in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> in 1674. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics and a navy. Accounts of the Maratha Empire credit him with the administrative system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashta-pradhan">Ashta Pradhan</a> council and the raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> in 1664.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shivaji.jpg" alt="Shivaji" width="298" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shivaji_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hindu chiefs and Maratha families, among them Shahaji Bhonsle, father of <b>Shivaji</b>, served as commanders and revenue officials, a dependence that later helped Shivaji build his own power in Bijapur's western territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The founder, <b>Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj</b> (1630–1680), son of the Bijapur commander Shahaji Bhonsle, used the rugged terrain of the Western Ghats and his grasp of guerrilla warfare to seize strategically placed forts such as Torna, Rajgad and later Raigad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1638 the governor of Surat jailed Virji Vora until Shah Jahan summoned him to court; Aurangzeb taxed Hindu merchants at 5 per cent against 2.5 for Muslims; and <b>Shivaji</b>’s raid on Surat in 1664 plundered Virji Vora’s house and warehouses.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In a lavish ceremony at Raigad fort, before nearly fifty thousand people, <b>Shivaji</b> was crowned king as Gaga Bhatt poured over him the waters of seven sacred rivers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shivaji</b> killed the Bijapur general Afzal Khan, and his Maratha forces defeated the Bijapur troops at Pratapgad, the Marathas' first significant military triumph against a major regional power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shivaji</b> and his son Sambhaji both failed to take Janjira, despite building the rival fort of Padmadurg nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/janjira-state">Janjira State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shivneri Fort, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Raigad Fort, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.2,73.86&amp;year=1662" aria-label="Shivneri Fort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6547 1452 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shivaji"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6997 1722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6997 1722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.2,73.86&amp;year=1662">What was here in 1662? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
